WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
VOICE OVER What is consciousness?&nbsp;
And how can we better understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:10.237
what our brains are actually doing when&nbsp;
they are experiencing consciousness.

00:00:10.237 --> 00:00:12.900
CHRISTOPHER TIMMERMANN It's&nbsp;
one of those early stages of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.520
scientific development in which we&nbsp;
are still agreeing on the concepts.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:23.340
VO Historically consciousness has been studied&nbsp;
by comparison to unconscious states like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:28.050
dreamless sleep - but some researchers&nbsp;
are interested in another perspective.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:32.520
CHRIS We try to understand how do we&nbsp;
change the quality of conscious experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.680
in a radically different way

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:40.200
VO One way to alter consciousness is&nbsp;
through the use of psychedelic drugs.&nbsp;

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:45.223
These are compounds that can change a&nbsp;
person's experience of consciousness.

00:00:45.223 --> 00:00:52.200
SHAMIL CHANDARIA Vast vistas, infinite&nbsp;
expanses of detail and intensity.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:59.520
VO And studying the effects these chemicals&nbsp;
have on the brain might be key not just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.460
unlocking our understanding&nbsp;
of how consciousness works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:07.200
but also to treating some of the problems&nbsp;
that can affect our waking lives.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.120
CHRIS What's intriguing is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.920
it appears that there's a wide plethora&nbsp;
of possibilities of applications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.920
and that's why we think that psychedelics&nbsp;
may be acting at some form of core mechanism

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.718
CHRIS We think of Consciousness simply&nbsp;
as experience. Right, so that

00:01:32.718 --> 00:01:37.858
that remains when we are not in dreamless&nbsp;
sleep or under anesthesia.

00:01:37.858 --> 00:01:44.280
We think that consciousness in terms of human&nbsp;
experience needs to contemplate also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:49.440
the alteration of consciousness while&nbsp;
we are still experiencing something

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:55.800
VO Chris Timmermann’s research uses psychedelic&nbsp;
substances to disrupt some of the key aspects of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:01.552
the human mind - like throwing a stone&nbsp;
into a still pond to see what happens.

00:02:01.552 --> 00:02:04.620
CHRIS perception makes up our&nbsp;
day-to-day experience of reality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:11.520
In the psychedelic state the features of&nbsp;
perception, our features of what constitute the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:17.460
external world are modified so for example with&nbsp;
this experiments that we do with DMT we see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:24.300
people have a perception of entering if you&nbsp;
will alternate dimensions that feel very real.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:32.100
All right so, so subjectively felt to people as&nbsp;
very strong. Modern neuroscientific theories uh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:37.200
posit that somehow we live in some form of&nbsp;
constrained hallucination, we make up reality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:43.980
So intriguingly enough in the psychedelic&nbsp;
state we may be able to access some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:51.900
brain mechanisms that allow us to construct&nbsp;
realities even our ordinary reality.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:56.640
VO To perturb people’s perceptions - as&nbsp;
well as other elements of consciousness in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.820
the brain - Chris needed a suitable&nbsp;
drug. His current focus is DMT or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:09.060
N,N-Dimethyltryptamine - a naturally occurring&nbsp;
compound found in small amounts in many plants.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:16.260
DMT is one of the active ingredients in&nbsp;
ayahuasca, the brew that has been prepared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:22.260
by indigenous peoples of the Amazon basin for&nbsp;
social and religious uses for over 1000 years.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:28.050
When it comes to research, DMT has several&nbsp;
advantages over other psychedelics.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:35.580
CHRIS With DMT we have a very reliable way to&nbsp;
induce a transformation of experience in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:46.020
external reality is radically transformed. What&nbsp;
feels like a real new sense of reality comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:55.560
up, emerges, that feels very immersive and the&nbsp;
duration, so this entry into this state lasts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:00.840
only 10 minutes so here we have an opportunity&nbsp;
to have someone in the scanner for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:06.240
administer DMT and capture their brain&nbsp;
activity throughout the whole process.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.300
VO The next challenge was finding participants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.960
willing to undergo this intense&nbsp;
experience under observation.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:19.020
SHAMIL I've been a long-term&nbsp;
meditator for 30 years and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:22.740
I'm very interested in the nature of&nbsp;
consciousness and the nature of self&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:30.360
and I started to hear about some of the research&nbsp;
that was being done in psychedelics and there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:38.460
was this whole renaissance uh in the last 10&nbsp;
15 years I was sort of drawn to try it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:44.400
uh and even though I'm over 50 I thought well&nbsp;
you know this is actually a good time to try it.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:49.140
VO Shamil was so interested in the new&nbsp;
research into psychedelic medicine that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.098
he helped to set up the The Centre&nbsp;
for Psychedelic Research at

00:04:52.098 --> 00:04:56.160
Imperial College London - which is how&nbsp;
he learned about Chris' work.

00:04:56.160 --> 00:05:00.000
SHAMIL As I became involved with&nbsp;
the centre I knew that Chris was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.580
working on this DMT study and I&nbsp;
thought it was very interesting.

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.760
VO Having been judged a suitable trial candidate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:15.240
Shamil was eventually prepared to receive a&nbsp;
dose of DMT while having his brain scanned.

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:19.800
SHAMIL And uh yeah I mean I was&nbsp;
definitely nervous by that stage.

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:24.720
CHRIS We're scanning the person&nbsp;
before the drug is injected,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:30.780
we scan them during the injection, we&nbsp;
scan them throughout the whole experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:36.510
and also when they're back. Participants&nbsp;
describe a range of different phenomena.

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:45.180
SHAMIL I get this incredible energy, vibrations&nbsp;
going throughout the body, and in the visual field&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:54.000
I feel the entire visual field pixelate&nbsp;
into fractals, into geometrical patterns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.840 --> 00:06:03.600
The body kind of dissolves in this enormous&nbsp;
Rush of energy you go up into realms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:13.800
You know, very difficult to describe, super&nbsp;
high definition moving patterns and geometry.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:19.980
CHRIS After that we perform interviews,&nbsp;
questionnaires, we capture psychological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:25.260
data or what we call phenomenological data&nbsp;
that pertains the study of human experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:31.620
so that then we can interpret whatever change&nbsp;
or modification we see in brain activity in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.620 --> 00:06:37.200
light of experience. The Intriguing&nbsp;
thing is that this experience many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:42.780
times for the individuals feel very&nbsp;
authentic, very real, it's almost as if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:48.660
an experience that they never thought&nbsp;
their mind would be able to conjure up.

00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:54.540
VO But what do these experiences tell us about&nbsp;
how the brain actually processes consciousness?

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:59.940
CHRIS We know that different brain&nbsp;
oscillations govern brain function,

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:04.800
right, and different brain waves are related&nbsp;
to different states of consciousness.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.800
VO The electrical activity of our brains can&nbsp;
be measured to reveal neural oscillations - the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.240
different patterns that emerge as networks&nbsp;
of synapses communicate with each other.

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:20.400
These brain waves change depending&nbsp;
on what our brain is doing. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:24.360
instance low frequency delta waves&nbsp;
come to the fore when we sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:28.500
while gamma waves become more&nbsp;
pronounced when we are concentrating.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:33.000
Alpha waves normally emerge&nbsp;
when we have our eyes closed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.860
and dampen down when we open&nbsp;
them - but DMT changes this.

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:42.300
CHRIS: DMT comes in and this Alpha wave goes&nbsp;
down but the eyes are still closed but nonetheless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:47.100
they're having a very vivid experience at the&nbsp;
sensory level and then the other one is that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:53.640
see that the lower frequencies, lower oscillations&nbsp;
when it comes to brainwave activity such as delta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.640 --> 00:08:00.660
waves which are also very pronounced in states&nbsp;
of sleeping. We see that also they become elevated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:07.320
And when you think a bit about what is really&nbsp;
occurring in in dreams and in sleeping is very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:15.060
similar we are detached from this external world&nbsp;
but nonetheless we start playing these movies in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:21.240
our in our in our brains in our minds making&nbsp;
up convincing reality, convincing experiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:23.760
very much as what happens in dreams.

00:08:23.760 --> 00:08:31.200
VO And to further understand the effects of&nbsp;
DMT, Chris is using fMRI - a technique better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:37.140
at pinpointing what is happening in specific&nbsp;
locations and specific networks within the brain.

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:41.640
CHRIS I think we can understand&nbsp;
these findings in light of the notion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:47.280
that consciousness is this form&nbsp;
of constrained hallucination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:55.380
and that this form of constraint hallucination is&nbsp;
governed by the hierarchy of these brain systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.380 --> 00:09:01.560
how they are arranged, and more importantly also&nbsp;
what is happening at the top of this hierarchy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:07.440
The systems that govern all the rest of the&nbsp;
connectivity patterns all the way down when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:14.520
it comes to human experience. All the way down&nbsp;
even into our more basic perceptual levels. Right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:21.230
So the idea is that models govern our perception&nbsp;
of what is real and what is not real.

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:30.660
Apparently with psychedelics when you disrupt these models at&nbsp;
this very high level everything down also can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:36.566
altered in a very significant way the brain is&nbsp;
able now to explore new models, new stories,

00:09:36.566 --> 00:09:43.140
new possibilities that can have the level of immersion&nbsp;
and reality that our everyday lives also have.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:48.480
VO Understanding how these drugs act on&nbsp;
the brain, and the effects they cause to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:54.240
the systems that underpin our consciousness opens&nbsp;
the door to some exciting practical applications.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:59.760
CHRIS When you think about depression or anxiety&nbsp;
one of the stable features is the symptom of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:07.320
rumination, in which bad stories are replayed,&nbsp;
and we cannot really break away from them, right&nbsp;&nbsp;.

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:13.320
There's a, there's kind of like a looping aspect to&nbsp;
them. If psychedelics effectively enable the system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:22.140
to more flexibly adapt into different models&nbsp;
that may be more healthy then we could expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:30.720
um that these substances have strong uses when&nbsp;
it comes to depression and anxiety for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:38.220
things that have rumination or other conditions in&nbsp;
which the idea of maladaptic models are presents

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:44.400
VO DMT shows incredible potential,&nbsp;
both as a clinical treatment and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:48.840
tool for research - and next Chris&nbsp;
wants to better harness the drug,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:53.820
customising and extending its&nbsp;
effects to reap even greater rewards.

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:58.440
CHRIS When we think about personalized medicine&nbsp;
and we think that every individual have their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:04.785
needs for example we can now think of tailoring&nbsp;
that experience to that individual for a

00:11:04.785 --> 00:11:10.560
a specific mental health purpose. And from the point&nbsp;
of view of consciousness research it provides us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:19.080
also a flexible way to explore the ways in&nbsp;
which we induce the alterations of experience and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:24.060
therefore try to address some of these fundamental&nbsp;
questions that concern human experience.

